WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.527
A useful way to learn NumPy is to think of it as a workflow.

00:00:04.527 --> 00:00:06.402
First, we create numeric data.

00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:12.652
That might come from a Python list, a simulation, a file, or another library.

00:00:12.652 --> 00:00:15.507
Second, we inspect the array.

00:00:15.507 --> 00:00:22.982
Before doing calculations, we ask questions such as: what is the shape, what data type is being stored,

00:00:22.982 --> 00:00:25.497
and how many dimensions do we have?

00:00:25.497 --> 00:00:29.432
Third, we transform the values using vectorized calculations.

00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:34.992
Fourth, we select the values that matter using indexing, slicing, or Boolean masks.

00:00:34.992 --> 00:00:42.007
As our data becomes more structured, we may reshape it into rows and columns and use axes to

00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:48.127
summarize specific dimensions. Then we calculate descriptive statistics or generate simulated data.

00:00:48.127 --> 00:00:51.382
Finally, we validate our assumptions.

00:00:51.382 --> 00:00:53.237
Does the shape match what we expected?

00:00:53.237 --> 00:01:00.442
Are there missing values? Did an operation modify the original array?

00:01:00.442 --> 00:01:05.662
This workflow is useful because many NumPy problems are easier to solve when we first understand the structure

00:01:05.662 --> 00:01:08.252
of the data.
